.TH crypt "" "Command"
.SH NAME
crypt \- encrypt/decrypt text
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcrypt [\fIpassword\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
crypt -- encrypt/decrypt text
Usage:	crypt [ password ]
Reads from standard input and writes to standard output.
Password is 10 characters or fewer.  The same password encrypts and decrypts.
.HE
.B crypt
reads the standard input, enciphers or deciphers it under
.IR password ,
and writes the result to the standard output.
The cipher is symmetric, so running
.B crypt
a second time over the output, with the same
.IR password ,
recovers the original text.
.PP
If
.I password
is not given as an argument,
.B crypt
prompts for it at the terminal without echoing what is typed.
An empty password typed at the prompt is rejected; only the first 10
characters of
.I password
are significant.
Once the password has been copied out of the argument vector (or out
of the prompt buffer), that copy is overwritten with nulls so the
plaintext password does not linger in memory.
.PP
Internally,
.B crypt
pads the password to 10 characters with a fixed built-in key, forces
the last two characters of the padded key into the range legal for a
password `salt', and passes the result through the system
password-hashing routine
.BR crypt() .
The last 11 characters of that hash become the working key.
The first 6 characters of the working key seed a pseudo-random shuffle
of a 256-byte substitution table (a self-inverse `rotor'); the last 6
characters seed a second pseudo-random sequence that supplies a
running offset.
Each byte read from the standard input is combined with the next
offset from that sequence, passed through the rotor, and the result
is written to the standard output.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B crypt
exits with status 1 if it is called with more than one argument, or if
no password is given on the command line and an empty one is typed at
the prompt.
.SH NOTES
Giving
.I password
as a command-line argument exposes it to anyone who can list the
process table while
.B crypt
is starting; the terminal prompt is the safer way to supply it.
